<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="practice-area-between-curves">

  <title>Area Between Curves</title>

  <exercise xml:id="practice-area-between-curves-1">
    <title>Area of a Bounded Region</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up and evaluate an integral to find the area of the region
        bounded by
        <m>y=x^2-2x</m>
        and
        <m>y=-x+2</m>.
        Sketch the region.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The curves intersect where
      </p>

      <me>
        x^2-2x=-x+2.
      </me>

      <p>
        Thus,
      </p>

      <me>
        x^2-x-2=(x-2)(x+1)=0,
      </me>

      <p>
        so the intersection points occur at <m>x=-1</m> and
        <m>x=2</m>. Between these points, the line
        <m>y=-x+2</m> lies above the parabola
        <m>y=x^2-2x</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Therefore, the area is
      </p>

      <me>
        A
        =
        \int_{-1}^{2}
        \left[(-x+2)-(x^2-2x)\right]\,dx
        =
        \int_{-1}^{2}
        (-x^2+x+2)\,dx.
      </me>

      <p>
        Evaluating,
      </p>

      <me>
        A
        =
        \left[
          -\frac{x^3}{3}
          +\frac{x^2}{2}
          +2x
        \right]_{-1}^{2}
        =
        \frac{9}{2}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Thus, the area of the bounded region is
        <m>\frac{9}{2}</m> square units.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

</section>